American culture includes music, art, literature, and film that have influenced the world. Jazz, rock and roll, and hip-hop originated in America. Hollywood has produced films watched globally. American literature includes famous authors like Mark Twain and Ernest Hemingway. American food reflects diverse immigrant influences including Italian, Chinese, Mexican, and many other cuisines.

Holidays like Independence Day (July 4th), Thanksgiving, and Memorial Day celebrate American values and history. The diversity of American culture makes it dynamic and constantly evolving. Americans celebrate their differences while working toward shared values. Understanding American culture helps explain the nation''s history and contemporary society.', '[{"definition":"Belief that anyone can achieve success through hard work and determination","image_url":null,"text":"American Dream"},{"definition":"Blending of cultures from immigrants creating unique American identity","image_url":null,"text":"Cultural Diversity"},{"definition":"Core American values including speech, religion, thought, and individual rights","image_url":null,"text":"Values of Freedom"},{"definition":"Days celebrating American history and values like Independence Day and Thanksgiving","image_url":null,"text":"National Holidays"}]'::jsonb, 5),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1048', 'Geography and Symbols', 'Capital Cities and Important Places', 'Learn about Washington D.C. and other significant American cities', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/3Z7GAPEt4tc', 'Washington, D.C., is the capital of the United States and home to the federal government. The city was designed by Pierre Charles L''Enfant with wide streets and organized layout. The Capitol Building houses Congress. The White House is the residence and office of the President. The Supreme Court Building is where the nation''s highest court meets.

Washington also features many monuments and memorials honoring American history. The Washington Monument is a tall obelisk honoring the first President. The Lincoln Memorial honors Abraham Lincoln. The Jefferson Memorial honors Thomas Jefferson. The World War II Memorial honors veterans. The Vietnam Veterans Memorial lists names of fallen soldiers.

Other important cities include New York City, the largest city and a major financial center. Boston is historically significant and home to major universities. Philadelphia hosted the Constitutional Convention. Chicago is a major industrial and commercial center. Los Angeles is known for entertainment and technology. These cities represent different aspects of American history, culture, and economy.', '[{"definition":"Capital of the United States, home to Congress, President, and Supreme Court","image_url":null,"text":"Washington, D.C."},{"definition":"Building where Congress meets to make federal laws","image_url":null,"text":"Capitol Building"},{"definition":"Residence and office of the President of the United States","image_url":null,"text":"White House"},{"definition":"Structures honoring important historical figures and events","image_url":null,"text":"Monuments and Memorials"}]'::jsonb, 4),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1047', 'Geography and Symbols', 'Natural Landmarks and National Parks', 'Explore America''s natural wonders and protected spaces', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/XJasV-itdoc', 'The United States has many spectacular natural landmarks that showcase its geographic diversity. The Grand Canyon in Arizona is one of the world''s most impressive geological formations. Niagara Falls on the border with Canada is a powerful waterfall. Yellowstone in Wyoming was the first national park and contains geysers and hot springs.

The Great Lakes are the world''s largest freshwater lake system. The Rocky Mountains stretch across western states. The Mississippi River is the nation''s longest river and was crucial to American history and commerce. The Appalachian Mountains run along the eastern United States. Yosemite National Park in California features granite cliffs and waterfalls.

National parks were established to protect America''s natural heritage for future generations. The National Park Service manages these protected areas. Millions of Americans visit parks annually for recreation, education, and natural beauty. Parks support wildlife conservation and scientific research. Natural landmarks are sources of national pride and connection to the land. Protecting these areas is considered a responsibility to future generations.', '[{"definition":"Spectacular natural landmark in Arizona formed by the Colorado River","image_url":null,"text":"Grand Canyon"},{"definition":"Protected areas preserving natural beauty and wildlife for public use","image_url":null,"text":"National Parks"},{"definition":"First national park established in 1872 known for geysers and wildlife","image_url":null,"text":"Yellowstone"},{"definition":"World''s largest freshwater lake system on the U.S.-Canada border","image_url":null,"text":"Great Lakes"}]'::jsonb, 3),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1046', 'Geography and Symbols', 'U.S. Geography and Regions', 'Understand the geography and regions that make up the United States', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/5g-vFOAfVT8', 'The United States spans from the Atlantic Ocean on the east to the Pacific Ocean on the west. The country can be divided into major regions with distinct geography, climate, and characteristics. The Northeast includes states like New York and Massachusetts with historic cities and manufacturing. The South includes states with warm climates, agriculture, and a unique culture.

The Midwest includes agricultural states in the heartland. The West includes mountain ranges, deserts, and vast territories. The Pacific Northwest has forests and rainy climate. The Southwest has deserts and canyons. Alaska is the largest state by area, while Hawaii is the most isolated. The United States has diverse geography including the Rocky Mountains, the Mississippi River, the Grand Canyon, and Niagara Falls.

Geography has influenced American history and development. Rivers provided transportation and power. Natural resources like coal, oil, and minerals supported industry. Mountains and natural barriers affected settlement patterns. Understanding American geography helps understand its history, economy, and diversity. Each region contributes uniquely to the nation.', '[{"definition":"Region including New York, Pennsylvania, and New England with historic cities and industry","image_url":null,"text":"Northeast Region"},{"definition":"Agricultural heartland region including farming and manufacturing states","image_url":null,"text":"Midwest Region"},{"definition":"Region with warm climate, agriculture, and distinct cultural heritage","image_url":null,"text":"South Region"},{"definition":"Region with mountains, deserts, and vast territories including Alaska and Hawaii","image_url":null,"text":"West Region"}]'::jsonb, 2),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1045', 'Geography and Symbols', 'American Symbols of Freedom and Democracy', 'Learn about iconic symbols representing American values', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/tjTWgTcW9nI', 'The United States has many powerful symbols that represent its values, history, and ideals. The American Flag features thirteen stripes representing the original colonies and stars representing each state. Red represents valor and bravery, white represents innocence and purity, and blue represents vigilance, perseverance, and justice.

The Bald Eagle, our national bird, symbolizes strength, courage, and independence. The Great Seal features an eagle holding an olive branch and arrows, symbolizing peace and defense. The Statue of Liberty, gifted by France in 1886, stands in New York Harbor welcoming immigrants and representing freedom and opportunity.

The Liberty Bell, located in Philadelphia, symbolizes American independence and freedom. The National Anthem, ''The Star-Spangled Banner,'' written in 1814, is played before national events. These symbols unite Americans and remind us of shared values. Schools display the flag and recite the Pledge of Allegiance. Americans cherish these symbols as representations of their nation and its ideals.', '[{"definition":"National flag with 13 stripes and 50 stars representing the states","image_url":null,"text":"American Flag"},{"definition":"National bird symbolizing strength, courage, and independence","image_url":null,"text":"Bald Eagle"},{"definition":"Monument in New York Harbor symbolizing freedom and hope","image_url":null,"text":"Statue of Liberty"},{"definition":"Historic bell in Philadelphia symbolizing American independence","image_url":null,"text":"Liberty Bell"}]'::jsonb, 1),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1044', 'U.S. History', 'Industrial Revolution and Modern America', 'Explore the transformation of America through industrialization and progress', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/xLhNP0qp38Q', 'The Industrial Revolution transformed America from a rural agricultural society to an urban industrial nation. Factories, railroads, and new technologies created enormous wealth and opportunities. Millions of immigrants came to America seeking jobs and better lives. Cities grew rapidly as people moved from farms to factories.

Industrialization created new social problems including poor working conditions, child labor, and urban poverty. Workers began organizing into unions to demand better wages, hours, and conditions. The Progressive Era (1890s-1920s) brought reforms including workplace safety laws, food safety regulations, and antitrust laws to prevent monopolies.

Technology advanced rapidly with the invention of electricity, automobiles, and airplanes. These innovations transformed daily life and commerce. The United States became an industrial power and eventually a global superpower. The 20th century saw America become a center of innovation, culture, and wealth. Industrial progress improved living standards for many Americans, though inequality and social problems persist.', '[{"definition":"Transformation of America from agricultural to industrial economy","image_url":null,"text":"Industrial Revolution"},{"definition":"Organizations of workers demanding better wages, hours, and working conditions","image_url":null,"text":"Labor Unions"},{"definition":"Period from 1890s-1920s of social and political reforms","image_url":null,"text":"Progressive Era"},{"definition":"Development of electricity, automobiles, and other inventions transforming society","image_url":null,"text":"Technology Innovation"}]'::jsonb, 5),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1043', 'U.S. History', 'Westward Expansion and Manifest Destiny', 'Understand the expansion of the United States across the continent', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/Q16OZkgSXfM', 'Manifest Destiny was the belief that American expansion across the continent was inevitable and justified. From the 1800s, Americans moved westward seeking land, opportunity, and resources. This expansion had profound effects on Native Americans and shaped the nation''s geography.

The Louisiana Purchase in 1803 doubled the size of the United States. The Oregon Territory was acquired through negotiation with Britain. The Mexican-American War (1846-1848) resulted in the United States gaining the Southwest and California. The California Gold Rush of 1849 brought thousands of settlers westward seeking fortune.

Westward expansion brought settlers into conflict with Native Americans, leading to wars and forced relocations. The Homestead Act of 1862 gave settlers free land in exchange for living and farming it. Railroads were built across the continent, connecting East and West. By 1890, the frontier was considered closed as settlement reached the Pacific. This expansion made the United States a continental power but came at great cost to Native Americans.', '[{"definition":"Belief that American expansion westward was inevitable and justified","image_url":null,"text":"Manifest Destiny"},{"definition":"1803 acquisition of territory from France that doubled United States size","image_url":null,"text":"Louisiana Purchase"},{"definition":"1846-1848 war resulting in U.S. acquisition of Southwest and California","image_url":null,"text":"Mexican-American War"},{"definition":"1862 law providing free land to settlers who lived and farmed it","image_url":null,"text":"Homestead Act"}]'::jsonb, 4),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1042', 'U.S. History', 'The Civil War and Abolition of Slavery', 'Learn about the Civil War and the end of slavery in America', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/k6N2P5yM0oU', 'The Civil War (1861-1865) was fought between the Union (North) and the Confederacy (South). The primary cause was disagreement over slavery. Southern states depended on slavery for their economy, while Northern states were moving toward free labor. When Abraham Lincoln was elected President in 1860, Southern states seceded from the Union.

President Lincoln led the North in a war to preserve the Union and end slavery. The Emancipation Proclamation in 1863 declared slaves in Confederate states free, though it wasn''t fully enforced until the North won the war. The 13th Amendment abolished slavery throughout the United States in 1865.

The Civil War killed over 600,000 soldiers and was the deadliest war in American history. General Ulysses S. Grant led the Union armies to victory. After the war, Reconstruction policies helped former slaves gain political power and education. The 14th and 15th Amendments provided equal protection and voting rights regardless of race. These changes fundamentally transformed American society.', '[{"definition":"War from 1861-1865 between Union (North) and Confederacy (South) over slavery","image_url":null,"text":"Civil War"},{"definition":"President who led the North and issued the Emancipation Proclamation","image_url":null,"text":"Abraham Lincoln"},{"definition":"1863 decree declaring slaves in Confederate states free","image_url":null,"text":"Emancipation Proclamation"},{"definition":"Constitutional amendment that abolished slavery in 1865","image_url":null,"text":"13th Amendment"}]'::jsonb, 3),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1041', 'U.S. History', 'American Independence and the Revolutionary War', 'Discover how the American colonies gained independence from Great Britain', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/NdRuU5ON-LU', 'American independence began with growing tensions between the thirteen British colonies and the British government in the late 1700s. The colonists felt unfairly taxed without representation in Parliament, leading to the famous phrase ''no taxation without representation.'' Key events leading to independence included the Boston Tea Party in 1773, where colonists dumped British tea into Boston Harbor to protest taxes.

On July 4, 1776, the Continental Congress in Philadelphia adopted the Declaration of Independence, written primarily by Thomas Jefferson. This document declared the colonies'' separation from Britain and outlined fundamental rights to ''life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.'' The Revolutionary War lasted from 1775 to 1783, with General George Washington commanding the Continental Army.

Key battles included Lexington and Concord in 1775 and Yorktown in 1781. France provided crucial support to the Americans. The Treaty of Paris in 1783 officially recognized American independence. The war established the United States as a new nation committed to democracy and individual rights.', '[{"definition":"Document adopted July 4, 1776, declaring American independence from Britain","image_url":null,"text":"Declaration of Independence"},{"definition":"Conflict from 1775-1783 resulting in American independence","image_url":null,"text":"Revolutionary War"},{"definition":"Commander of Continental Army and first President of the United States","image_url":null,"text":"George Washington"},{"definition":"1773 protest against British taxes by colonists dumping tea into Boston Harbor","image_url":null,"text":"Boston Tea Party"}]'::jsonb, 2),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1040', 'U.S. History', 'Native Americans and European Colonization', 'Explore the early history of America and the impact on Native peoples', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/TTYOQ05oDOI', 'Long before European arrival, Native Americans lived in North America for thousands of years, developing diverse civilizations, cultures, and governments. They had their own laws, trade systems, and societies. When Europeans arrived in the late 1400s, they were seeking new trade routes and resources.

European colonization had devastating effects on Native Americans. Diseases brought from Europe killed millions of Native Americans who had no immunity. Europeans took Native American lands and forced them onto smaller areas called reservations. Native Americans were often treated as inferior and had few legal rights. Many were forced to abandon their languages, religions, and cultures.

Despite these hardships, Native American cultures survived and continue today. There are 574 federally recognized tribes in the United States. Native Americans have fought for justice and recognition. The Indian Removal Act of 1830 forced thousands of Native Americans west on the deadly Trail of Tears. Today, Native Americans advocate for sovereignty, cultural preservation, and justice for historical wrongs.', '[{"definition":"Diverse indigenous societies with sophisticated governments and cultures predating European arrival","image_url":null,"text":"Native American Civilizations"},{"definition":"Process of European settlement in America starting in late 1400s","image_url":null,"text":"European Colonization"},{"definition":"Areas of land designated by government for Native Americans","image_url":null,"text":"Reservations"},{"definition":"Forced migration of Native Americans westward in the 1830s resulting in thousands of deaths","image_url":null,"text":"Trail of Tears"}]'::jsonb, 1),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d103f', 'Rights and Responsibilities', 'Due Process and Justice', 'Learn about the legal protections that ensure fair treatment in the justice system', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/J436pFZA3is', 'Due process is the right to fair treatment in the legal system. The Constitution guarantees that government cannot take away life, liberty, or property without due process. This means following established legal procedures and laws. Due process protections ensure that a person cannot be arrested without cause or punished without a fair trial.

Criminal due process includes the right to know the charges, the right to an attorney, the right to a fair trial, and protection against self-incrimination. The Fifth Amendment states that no person shall be deprived of life, liberty, or property without due process. The Sixth Amendment guarantees the right to a fair and speedy trial with a lawyer.

Innocent until proven guilty is a fundamental principle. The burden of proof is on the government to prove guilt beyond reasonable doubt. People have the right to challenge evidence against them and to have a lawyer defend them. These protections apply even to people accused of serious crimes. The system isn''t perfect, but these procedures help prevent innocent people from being wrongly punished.', '[{"definition":"Right to fair legal procedures and protection of rights under law","image_url":null,"text":"Due Process"},{"definition":"Protects against self-incrimination and guarantees due process","image_url":null,"text":"Fifth Amendment"},{"definition":"Guarantees right to fair trial and legal representation","image_url":null,"text":"Sixth Amendment"},{"definition":"Legal principle that government must prove guilt beyond reasonable doubt","image_url":null,"text":"Innocent Until Proven Guilty"}]'::jsonb, 5),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d103e', 'Rights and Responsibilities', 'Balancing Rights and Responsibilities', 'Explore how individual rights and community responsibilities work together', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/m1r2Ui4UmiQ', 'Individual rights and community responsibilities must be balanced for society to function well. Your right to speak freely is limited when it harms others, such as shouting false information that causes panic. Your right to privacy is limited when you''re suspected of a crime. Your right to property is limited by zoning laws and environmental regulations.

Responsibilities protect the common good and allow others to enjoy their rights. Traffic laws restrict individual freedom but protect everyone''s safety. Public education is both a right and a responsibility—all children have the right to education, and society has responsibility to provide it. Environmental regulations restrict some business activities but protect everyone''s health.

Courts balance individual rights with community needs. The Constitution protects rights, but no right is absolute. A person can speak freely but cannot commit fraud. People can own guns but cannot use them to harm others. This balance is essential to democracy. Good citizens understand both their rights and their responsibilities to others.', '[{"definition":"Individual rights protected by Constitution while respecting others'' rights","image_url":null,"text":"Balanced Rights"},{"definition":"General welfare and benefit of the whole community","image_url":null,"text":"Common Good"},{"definition":"Constitutional rights that have reasonable limits to protect community welfare","image_url":null,"text":"Limited Rights"},{"definition":"Agreement where citizens accept responsibilities in exchange for rights and protection","image_url":null,"text":"Social Contract"}]'::jsonb, 4),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d103d', 'Rights and Responsibilities', 'Civil Rights and Equal Protection', 'Understand the legal protections ensuring equality for all Americans', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/qKK5KVI9_Q8', 'Civil rights are the rights guaranteed to all citizens regardless of race, color, religion, sex, or national origin. The 14th Amendment guarantees equal protection under the law and due process. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 prohibited discrimination in public places, employment, and education. The Voting Rights Act of 1965 eliminated barriers preventing minorities from voting.

The Fair Housing Act of 1968 prohibited housing discrimination. The Americans with Disabilities Act protects people with disabilities. Legal protections ensure that people cannot be denied services, jobs, or housing based on protected characteristics. These laws arose from the Civil Rights Movement of the 1950s and 1960s when African Americans fought for equal treatment.

Despite legal protections, discrimination can still occur. People can file complaints with government agencies when they experience discrimination. The Justice Department enforces civil rights laws. Courts have consistently upheld the principle that all people deserve equal treatment. Civil rights remain important as society works to ensure equality and dignity for all Americans.', '[{"definition":"Guarantees equal protection and due process to all citizens","image_url":null,"text":"14th Amendment"},{"definition":"Federal law prohibiting discrimination in public places, employment, and education","image_url":null,"text":"Civil Rights Act of 1964"},{"definition":"Federal law eliminating literacy tests and voting barriers for minorities","image_url":null,"text":"Voting Rights Act of 1965"},{"definition":"Legal guarantee that laws apply equally to all people without discrimination","image_url":null,"text":"Equal Protection"}]'::jsonb, 3),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d103c', 'Rights and Responsibilities', 'Responsibilities of American Citizens', 'Learn about the duties and responsibilities that maintain democracy', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/a2-QL59pVRo', 'With rights come responsibilities. Citizens have a duty to obey laws that apply equally to everyone. Paying taxes funds government services like schools, roads, defense, and welfare programs. Serving on juries when called ensures that trials are fair and justice is served by a group of peers.

Citizens should vote in elections to ensure their voices are heard and that leaders remain accountable. Educating oneself about issues and candidates helps ensure informed voting. Citizens can volunteer in their communities and participate in civic organizations. Respecting the rights and property of others is essential for a functioning society.

Parents have responsibility to educate their children. Citizens should respect the environment for future generations. Many believe citizens also have responsibility to understand the Constitution and their rights. These responsibilities help maintain the strong democracy that protects everyone''s rights. When citizens fulfill these responsibilities, government works more effectively and communities thrive.', '[{"definition":"Duty of citizens to participate in and support their government and community","image_url":null,"text":"Civic Responsibility"},{"definition":"Obligation to serve on juries when called, ensuring fair trials","image_url":null,"text":"Jury Duty"},{"definition":"Responsibility to pay taxes that fund government services and infrastructure","image_url":null,"text":"Tax Obligation"},{"definition":"Responsibility to learn about candidates and issues before voting","image_url":null,"text":"Informed Voting"}]'::jsonb, 2),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d103b', 'Rights and Responsibilities', 'Fundamental Rights of Citizens', 'Discover the rights that protect all Americans', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/nDgIVseTkuE', 'All American citizens have fundamental rights protected by the Constitution and laws. Freedom of speech allows Americans to express opinions without government interference. Freedom of religion allows everyone to practice their faith or no faith. Freedom of the press ensures that journalists can report information without government censorship.

Citizens also have the right to petition the government and assemble peacefully. The right to a fair trial protects against unjust punishment. Property rights allow citizens to own and use property. The right to vote ensures all eligible citizens can participate in elections. These rights apply equally to all citizens regardless of race, religion, gender, or background.

Many countries do not protect these fundamental rights, making them particularly valuable in America. The Constitution guarantees these rights, but citizens must be vigilant in protecting them. The Supreme Court interprets what these rights mean in modern contexts. Throughout history, Americans have fought to expand these rights to all citizens.', '[{"definition":"Right to express opinions and ideas without government interference","image_url":null,"text":"Freedom of Speech"},{"definition":"Right to practice any faith or no faith without government interference","image_url":null,"text":"Freedom of Religion"},{"definition":"Fundamental right of eligible citizens to participate in elections","image_url":null,"text":"Right to Vote"},{"definition":"Constitutional protection against unjust punishment and guarantee of due process","image_url":null,"text":"Right to a Fair Trial"}]'::jsonb, 1),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d103a', 'System of Government', 'State and Local Government', 'Understand how state and local governments serve citizens', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/_sQNz2liUuY', 'State governments follow a similar structure to the federal government with executive, legislative, and judicial branches. Each state has a Governor (executive), a state legislature (legislative), and state courts (judicial). State governments handle issues like education, public safety, transportation, and welfare within their borders.

Local governments include county, city, and township governments. A mayor typically leads a city government, while commissioners or supervisors run county governments. Local governments provide services like police, fire, water, sewer, and roads. Many decisions that directly affect daily life are made at the local level.

The relationship between federal, state, and local governments is defined by the Constitution and federal laws. States cannot contradict federal law, but they have freedom to govern on matters not reserved for the federal government. Citizens participate in all levels of government through voting and civic engagement. Local governments are often the most accessible to citizens and play a crucial role in American democracy.', '[{"definition":"Chief executive of a state government, similar to the President at federal level","image_url":null,"text":"Governor"},{"definition":"State lawmaking body, usually consisting of a state senate and house of representatives","image_url":null,"text":"State Legislature"},{"definition":"City and county governments providing services like police, fire, and infrastructure","image_url":null,"text":"Local Government"},{"definition":"Division of power and responsibilities among federal, state, and local governments","image_url":null,"text":"Federalism in Practice"}]'::jsonb, 5),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1039', 'System of Government', 'Checks and Balances', 'Learn how the three branches of government limit each other''s power', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/6W8W3p9Ot7M', 'The system of checks and balances ensures that no single branch of government becomes too powerful. Each branch has powers to limit the other branches. The Legislative Branch can override the President''s veto, refuse to pass the President''s budget, and remove the President through impeachment. The Senate confirms judges and cabinet appointees.

The Executive Branch (President) can veto laws passed by Congress and appoints judges and cabinet members. The President enforces laws made by Congress. The Judicial Branch can declare laws unconstitutional and interpret how laws apply. The Senate must confirm judicial appointments, and Congress can propose amendments to overturn Supreme Court decisions.

This system prevents concentration of power and protects citizens'' rights. The Founding Fathers deliberately created a government where each branch would compete and balance the others. Examples of checks and balances in action include Congress overriding a presidential veto, the Supreme Court declaring a law unconstitutional, or the Senate rejecting a presidential appointee. This design has kept America''s government stable for over 230 years.', '[{"definition":"System where each branch of government limits the power of the other branches","image_url":null,"text":"Checks and Balances"},{"definition":"Congress can pass a law with two-thirds vote despite presidential veto","image_url":null,"text":"Presidential Veto Override"},{"definition":"Process where Congress can formally charge a president or judge with wrongdoing","image_url":null,"text":"Impeachment"},{"definition":"Courts can declare laws unconstitutional, checking legislative power","image_url":null,"text":"Judicial Review"}]'::jsonb, 4),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1038', 'System of Government', 'The Judicial Branch and Supreme Court', 'Explore how courts interpret laws and protect constitutional rights', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/IGyx5UEwgtA', 'The Judicial Branch consists of federal courts headed by the Supreme Court of the United States. The Supreme Court has nine justices, including the Chief Justice. Judges are appointed by the President and confirmed by the Senate. Federal judges serve lifetime appointments, ensuring independence from political pressure.

The Supreme Court is the highest court in the nation and has the power of judicial review—the ability to declare laws unconstitutional. Lower federal courts handle various cases, and there are state court systems as well. The court system ensures that justice is applied fairly and that the Constitution is upheld.

The Supreme Court hears cases that involve constitutional questions or disputes between states. Justices write opinions explaining their decisions, which set legal precedents. The Court balances individual rights with government authority. Nine justices must agree on decisions, promoting careful deliberation. The judicial system protects Americans from unjust laws and ensures equal protection under law.', '[{"definition":"Highest federal court with nine justices, has power of judicial review","image_url":null,"text":"Supreme Court"},{"definition":"Power to declare laws unconstitutional if they violate the Constitution","image_url":null,"text":"Judicial Review"},{"definition":"Appointed by President, confirmed by Senate, serve lifetime appointments","image_url":null,"text":"Federal Judges"},{"definition":"Previous court decisions that guide how future cases are decided","image_url":null,"text":"Legal Precedent"}]'::jsonb, 3),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1037', 'System of Government', 'The Executive Branch and the Presidency', 'Understand the role and powers of the President and executive government', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/BWR2p9j9hWo', 'The President is the head of the Executive Branch and serves as commander-in-chief of the armed forces. The President is elected to four-year terms and can serve a maximum of two terms due to the 22nd Amendment. The President enforces federal laws, appoints federal judges and cabinet members, negotiates treaties, and proposes the federal budget to Congress.

The President''s Cabinet consists of department heads who advise the President and manage specific areas of government like defense, state, treasury, and interior. The Executive Office of the President includes the White House staff and advisors who assist with daily operations and policy decisions.

The President can veto legislation passed by Congress, but Congress can override this veto with a two-thirds vote. Presidents issue executive orders to direct government agencies. The President also represents the United States in foreign relations and ceremonial matters. Elections every four years ensure that the President remains accountable to the people.', '[{"definition":"Head of the Executive Branch, elected to four-year terms, limited to two terms maximum","image_url":null,"text":"President"},{"definition":"Group of department heads who advise the President and manage executive departments","image_url":null,"text":"Cabinet"},{"definition":"President''s power to reject legislation passed by Congress","image_url":null,"text":"Presidential Veto"},{"definition":"Directive issued by the President to direct government agencies and enforce laws","image_url":null,"text":"Executive Order"}]'::jsonb, 2),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1036', 'System of Government', 'Congress and the Legislative Branch', 'Learn how Congress makes laws and represents the American people', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/hltv8-nzcUc', 'Congress is the legislative branch of the U.S. government and is divided into two chambers: the Senate and the House of Representatives. The Senate has 100 members—two from each state, regardless of population. Senators serve six-year terms. The House has 435 members, with representation based on state population. House members serve two-year terms.

Congress has the power to make federal laws, approve budgets, declare war, and ratify treaties. Bills must pass both chambers and be signed by the President to become law. Congress can override a presidential veto with a two-thirds vote in both chambers. Members of Congress represent the interests of their constituents and help bring federal resources to their districts.

The Speaker of the House leads the House, while the Vice President is the President of the Senate. Congressional committees handle specific areas like defense, healthcare, and finance. Congress meets in the Capitol Building in Washington, D.C. Regular elections ensure that representatives remain accountable to the people.', '[{"definition":"Upper chamber of Congress with 100 members, two from each state serving six-year terms","image_url":null,"text":"Senate"},{"definition":"Lower chamber of Congress with 435 members based on state population, serving two-year terms","image_url":null,"text":"House of Representatives"},{"definition":"Proposed legislation that must pass both chambers and be signed by the President","image_url":null,"text":"Bills and Laws"},{"definition":"Groups focused on specific policy areas that draft and review legislation","image_url":null,"text":"Congressional Committees"}]'::jsonb, 1),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1035', 'Principles of American Democracy', 'Democracy and Federal Government Structure', 'Understand the principles that make America a democratic republic', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/lrk4oY7UxpQ', 'The United States is a democratic republic, meaning power ultimately rests with the people who elect representatives to govern. Democracy is based on the principle that all people are equal and have a say in government decisions.

A federal system divides power between the national government and state governments. This prevents any single authority from becoming too powerful. The national government handles matters affecting the whole country, while states handle local issues. This system allows for both unity and diversity across the nation.

America''s democracy relies on free and fair elections, freedom of the press, separation of powers, and protection of individual rights. Citizens participate through voting, serving on juries, and engaging in civic activities. A healthy democracy requires informed citizens who understand their rights and responsibilities. The Constitution protects these democratic principles and can be amended only through a lengthy process.', '[{"definition":"System where power rests with the people who elect representatives to govern","image_url":null,"text":"Democratic Republic"},{"definition":"Division of power between national and state governments","image_url":null,"text":"Federalism"},{"definition":"Division of government into three branches with distinct powers","image_url":null,"text":"Separation of Powers"},{"definition":"Core values including equality, representation, and protection of rights","image_url":null,"text":"Democratic Principles"}]'::jsonb, 5),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1034', 'Principles of American Democracy', 'Voting Rights and Democratic Participation', 'Learn how voting evolved and what voting rights mean in a democracy', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/sQcwHXgmR8M', 'Voting is a fundamental right in American democracy, but it wasn''t always available to everyone. Originally, only white male property owners could vote. Over time, voting rights have been expanded through amendments and legislation.

The 15th Amendment (1870) prohibited voting discrimination based on race. The 19th Amendment (1920) granted women the right to vote. The Voting Rights Act of 1965 eliminated literacy tests and other barriers used to prevent African Americans from voting. The 26th Amendment (1971) lowered the voting age to 18.

Today, U.S. citizens who are at least 18 years old and meet residency requirements can vote. Voting is done through elections where citizens choose representatives and leaders. Regular elections ensure that government remains accountable to the people. Early voting and mail-in ballots make it easier for citizens to participate in democracy.', '[{"definition":"Constitutional right of citizens to participate in elections and choose representatives","image_url":null,"text":"Voting Rights"},{"definition":"Prohibited voting discrimination based on race, ratified in 1870","image_url":null,"text":"15th Amendment"},{"definition":"1965 federal law that eliminated literacy tests and other voting barriers","image_url":null,"text":"Voting Rights Act"},{"definition":"Active involvement of citizens in the government process through voting and civic engagement","image_url":null,"text":"Democratic Participation"}]'::jsonb, 4),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1033', 'Principles of American Democracy', 'Individual Rights and Civil Liberties', 'Explore the freedoms and rights that protect all Americans', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/kbwsF-A2sTg', 'The Bill of Rights protects ten fundamental freedoms that are essential to American democracy. The First Amendment guarantees freedom of speech, religion, press, assembly, and petition. These freedoms allow Americans to express themselves and participate in democratic processes.

The Second Amendment protects the right to bear arms. The Third Amendment prevents quartering of soldiers in homes. The Fourth Amendment protects against unreasonable searches and seizures. The Fifth Amendment provides due process rights and prevents self-incrimination. The Sixth Amendment guarantees the right to a fair trial.

The Seventh Amendment provides for jury trials in civil cases, and the Eighth Amendment prohibits cruel and unusual punishment. The Ninth Amendment protects rights not specifically listed, and the Tenth Amendment reserves powers to the states or people. These rights apply equally to all citizens and cannot be taken away without legal process.', '[{"definition":"Protects freedom of speech, religion, press, assembly, and petition","image_url":null,"text":"First Amendment"},{"definition":"Provides due process rights and protection against self-incrimination","image_url":null,"text":"Fifth Amendment"},{"definition":"Fair treatment through established judicial procedures and laws","image_url":null,"text":"Due Process"},{"definition":"Fundamental rights and freedoms protected by the Constitution","image_url":null,"text":"Civil Liberties"}]'::jsonb, 3),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1032', 'Principles of American Democracy', 'Amendments and Constitutional Changes', 'Understand how the Constitution evolves through amendments and what they mean', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/bO7Ho0vejfs', 'Amendments are formal changes to the Constitution and require approval by two-thirds of Congress and ratification by three-fourths of the states. The Constitution has been amended 27 times, showing how it adapts to changing times while maintaining core principles.

The Bill of Rights (First 10 amendments) protect fundamental freedoms. The 13th Amendment abolished slavery in 1865. The 14th Amendment, passed after the Civil War, guarantees equal protection and due process. The 15th Amendment prohibited voting discrimination based on race. The 19th Amendment granted women the right to vote in 1920. The 26th Amendment lowered the voting age to 18 in 1971.

Amendments can also expand voting rights, change how government operates, and respond to historical events. The lengthy amendment process ensures that changes are carefully considered and broadly supported by the nation.', '[{"definition":"Formal change to the Constitution requiring two-thirds Congress approval and three-fourths state ratification","image_url":null,"text":"Constitutional Amendment"},{"definition":"Abolished slavery throughout the United States in 1865","image_url":null,"text":"13th Amendment"},{"definition":"Granted women the right to vote, ratified in 1920","image_url":null,"text":"19th Amendment"},{"definition":"Lowered the voting age to 18 years old","image_url":null,"text":"26th Amendment"}]'::jsonb, 2),
  ('699db0a1297afea4b73d1031', 'Principles of American Democracy', 'The U.S. Constitution and the Founding Fathers', 'Learn about the creation of the Constitution and the visionary leaders who shaped America', 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/bO7FQsCcbD8', 'The U.S. Constitution was created in 1787 at the Constitutional Convention in Philadelphia. Fifty-five delegates from twelve states gathered to draft a document that would establish the framework for American government. The Constitution replaced the Articles of Confederation, which had proven too weak to effectively govern the new nation.

Key Founding Fathers included George Washington, James Madison, Benjamin Franklin, and James Wilson. Madison is often called the "Father of the Constitution" because he came prepared with detailed plans for the government structure. Franklin, at 81 years old, was the oldest delegate and provided wisdom and compromise throughout the convention.

The Constitution established three branches of government with a system of checks and balances. It also protected individual rights through the Bill of Rights, added in 1791. The Constitution has been amended 27 times and remains the supreme law of the land. It was ratified by the states in 1788 and went into effect in 1789.', '[{"definition":"Meeting of 55 delegates in Philadelphia in 1787 to draft the U.S. Constitution","image_url":null,"text":"Constitutional Convention"},{"definition":"Primary architect of the Constitution, known as the ''Father of the Constitution''","image_url":null,"text":"James Madison"},{"definition":"Process of state approval required for the Constitution to become the supreme law","image_url":null,"text":"Ratification"},{"definition":"First ten amendments protecting fundamental individual freedoms","image_url":null,"text":"Bill of Rights"}]'::jsonb, 1);





























